mat  ready for them to use  in manufacture  to support our clients and customers  with delivering their requirements  we have a dedicated program or roadmap  of work and processes the process  consists of three discrete phases and  i will go into each detail sorry i will  go into detail  on each of these phases later on at the  start of the project  using computational chemistry a number  of suitable ligands are identified  then in phase two an absorbent is  selected  and developed in parallel alongside the  identification of suitable conditions  and operational parameters this  culminates  in pinpointing a final adsorbent and  process  which is ready for phase three this  is where the absorbent is transferred to  manufacturing  accompanied with a full regulatory  support package  and thus supplying a custom adsorbent or  if required  a pre-pack column to enable the customer  to deliver  the first phase is to identify ligand  candidates  specific to the target and successive  screening  to narrow in on the top candidates for  absorpent and processed development  so how do we decide what route to take  what we know about the target and its  interactions  help us decide which strategy to follow  for ligand discovery  if the target protein structure is known  and available  and there is a known binding site we can  use ligand docking  and virtual high throughput screening  techniques  licking ligand docking means that we  take  selected ligands from our virtual  library and virtually place them  in the binding site and evaluate the fit  of the ligand  within the binding site alternatively  if the binding site is not known we will  use the binding site determination or  blind docking to identify ligands  suitable for the target protein  this is where the virtual ligand library  is screened in silico  against the protein to identify ligands  that have an affinity for the protein  binding site  when we don't have structural  information about the protein  we can use information about known  inhibitors and search for ligands with  structural similarities  of course there are also occasions when  there is no structural information  available  or known inhibitors to the target  protein  the ligand discovery approach then is  via the systematic  high throughput screening of a selection  of libraries or  the identification of a hit through a  more discrete but diverse library  as mentioned earlier i want to describe  a case study whereby we supported  one of our customers and supplying them  with a custom affinity adsorbent  in this case study we already knew the  structure of the target protein  we were also aware that there were  several different isoforms  of the target protein which the customer  ideally wanted the ligand  which could bind to all i.e a platform  technology  the feedstock which was supplied was a  clarified cell culture supernatant  containing the target isoforms but at  low concentrations  this added an extra layer of complexity  regarding the ligand discovery strategy  the following path was selected  as the target protein structure was  known and available  however the ligand binding site was not  a ligand binding site had to be  determined followed by docking studies  using computational chemistry  as we wanted a ligand that could be used  for multiple isoforms  of the target protein or proteins we  started by determining  common clefts accessible to the ligands  the surface of the protein was scanned  for possible ligand binding sites  and we used docking studies to dock the  ligand's ligand structures  to the possible binding sites as well  has determined the binding energy  overall the more negative the more  favorable however  a balance is needed to be struck whereby  we obtained  a good fit for binding ie selectivity  but not too tight as to not be able to  recover  the target material using conventional  illusion conditions  from these studies we selected a number  of possible candidates  however we needed to consider many  factors  ligand orientation is one as well as  certain restrictive factors  which can include cost ligand  availability  ligand toxicity as well as physical and  chemical restrictions  such as site accessibility and  the spacer arm which attaches the ligand  to the absorbent  the restrictive factors such as the  spacer arm  could have both a positive or negative  influence  on the binding if the spacer arm is too  hydrophobic  there could be repulsive forces which  could detrimentally affect the binding  or conversely certain charge groups or  the length of the spacer arm  may actually aid in the ligand docking  based on selective structures from the  virtual screening  we designed a library of triesine based  compounds  the library explored the chemical space  around  the selected structures it is useful to  constrain  the library so as to focus the screening  efforts  on this slide we have depicted the same  binding site  but two different candidates with  different structures  it is worth noting that the positioning  or orientation of the ligand  within the binding site is important to  allow the spacer arm to be attached  this is indicated with the circle  in both cases the ligands have an  nh3 group to couple onto the spacer arm  in  an acceptable position it is worth  highlighting of course  that in some cases the protein structure  is not known  in these cases work can be done by  initially exploring  a wider chemical space than narrowing  down the focus  as the results are generated this is  more  of an iterative approach a lot of time  is spent during this phase  to ensure that the right targets are  selected for generating  suitable libraries  this approach allows for the synthesis  of combinatorial libraries  which are ideal for identifying ligands  with mixed modes of action  the libraries generated are with  chrysine based  ligands which is the basis of most of  our ligands  trisene ligands are ligands which are  small  chemical constructs developed on a core  triazine ring  attached via a spacer arm onto an  agarose or other support  the ligand the ligand is built up by  activating the dichlorotriazine  then coupling a primary amine to the  trisene core  and creating an intermediate the  monochlorotriazine intermediate  is then further substituted to a final  two-dimensional ligand  astria standardly creates an 8x8 array  library  of subtly different ligands this  building  of ligand libraries using a wide range  of different chemical compounds  allows for the creation of an extensive  library  incorporating a wide variety of  properties  which are costly stable  the libraries generated are pure plates  which are comprised of 64 discrete  packed  micro columns with top and bottom frits  and reservoirs above  these columns will drain freely under  gravity so  automated liquid handling systems are  not required  only a multi-channel pipette  these pureplate libraries allow us to  perform  the screening like using a standard  column  allowing for equilibration sample  application  wash steps elution and cip steps  the fractions of interest are collected  by drainage into deep well collection  plates  which can be analyzed using discrete  analytical tools  such as sds page or by a variety of  plate-based assays  to measure the amount of target protein  or impurities in each fraction  this allows for a variety of factors to  be identified  for the suitable ligand which binds to  the target  once the screening is complete the  fraction samples test  the fraction samples are tested using a  range of assays  the data can then be presented as a heat  map  such like here this heat map can be  built up  based on predetermined criteria such as  binding recovery purity ease evolution  or others from these criteria  the best adsorbents are selected a heap  map  can have a nominal scoring of one to  five  when the factors based on factors which  don't have a clear defined value  assigned  such as ease of evolution or there could  be  a percentage value out of a hundred  whereby the higher the value the better  in this case the poor performing ligands  are  colored in blue and the best performing  ligands are colored in red  from the screening we select one or more  absorbance  for further development or we decide  design a new library based on these  results and repeat the process  in this case as there were several  high-performing candidates  additional scrutiny was applied to the  results to identify the top candidates  in order to proceed to the absorbent  development  once the first phase is complete and one  or more usually up to six adsorbents  depending on what was identified during  ligand discovery  and these candidates have been  identified the next phase begins  absorbent development and process  development  the absorbent development can be  effectively divided into two parts  the chemistry development and the  downstream processing  the chemistry development focuses on  many aspects  including the optimization of the space  rom  the length and how the how hydrophobic  or how hydrophilic it needs to be  as mentioned earlier the ligand density  investing investigating a range of  ligand density absorbance  to explore selectivity and capacity  ligand attachment chemistry the base  matrix selection  i previously mentioned that we couple  the ligand to purebead  which is our near monodispersed  agarose-based matrix material  however other base matrices or even  other supports  can be investigated rational  optimization  of the ligand and absorbance synthesis  for improving cost effectiveness  and process robustness is also carried  out and  the list continues for the chemistry  development  the other part is the downstream  processing this focuses  on the development of a suitable wash  elution  regeneration and cip conditions as well  as testing the performance  of the absorbance under the standard  process conditions  the downstream processing will perform  all