mation about known  inhibitors and search for ligands with  structural similarities  of course there are also occasions when  there is no structural information  available  or known inhibitors to the target  protein  the ligand discovery approach then is  via the systematic  high throughput screening of a selection  of libraries or  the identification of a hit through a  more discrete but diverse library  as mentioned earlier i want to describe  a case study whereby we supported  one of our customers and supplying them  with a custom affinity adsorbent  in this case study we already knew the  structure of the target protein  we were also aware that there were  several different isoforms  of the target protein which the customer  ideally wanted the ligand  which could bind to all i.e a platform  technology  the feedstock which was supplied was a  clarified cell culture supernatant  containing the target isoforms but at  low concentrations  this added an extra layer of complexity  regarding the ligand discovery strategy  the following path was selected  as the target protein structure was  known and available  however the ligand binding site was not  a ligand binding site had to be  determined followed by docking studies  using computational chemistry  as we wanted a ligand that could be used  for multiple isoforms  of the target protein or proteins we  started by determining  common clefts accessible to the ligands  the surface of the protein was scanned  for possible ligand binding sites  and we used docking studies to dock the  ligand's ligand structures  to the possible binding sites as well  has determined the binding energy  overall the more negative the more  favorable however  a balance is needed to be struck whereby  we obtained  a good fit for binding ie selectivity  but not too tight as to not be able to  recover  the target material using conventional  illusion conditions  from these studies we selected a number  of possible candidates  however we needed to consider many  factors  ligand orientation is one as well as  certain restrictive factors  which can include cost ligand  availability  ligand toxicity as well as physical and  chemical restrictions  such as site accessibility and  the spacer arm which attaches the ligand  to the absorbent  the restrictive factors such as the  spacer arm  could have both a positive or negative  influence  on the binding if the spacer arm is too  hydrophobic  there could be repulsive forces which  could detrimentally affect the binding  or conversely certain charge groups or  the length of the spacer arm  may actually aid in the ligand docking  based on selective structures from the  virtual screening  we designed a library of triesine based  compounds  the library explored the chemical space  around  the selected structures it is useful to  constrain  the library so as to focus the screening  efforts  on this slide we have depicted the same  binding site  but two different candidates with  different structures  it is worth noting that the positioning  or orientation of the ligand  within the binding site is important to  allow the spacer arm to be attached  this is indicated with the circle  in both cases the ligands have an  nh3 group to couple onto the spacer arm  in  an acceptable position it is worth  highlighting of course  that in some cases the protein structure  is not known  in these cases work can be done by  initially exploring  a wider chemical space than narrowing  down the focus  as the results are generated this is  more  of an iterative approach a lot of time  is spent during this phase  to ensure that the right targets are  selected for generating  suitable libraries  this approach allows for the synthesis  of combinatorial libraries  which are ideal for identifying ligands  with mixed modes of action  the libraries generated are with  chrysine based  ligands which is the basis of most of  our ligands  trisene ligands are ligands which are  small  chemical constructs developed on a core  triazine ring  attached via a spacer arm onto an  agarose or other support  the ligand the ligand is built up by  activating the dichlorotriazine  then coupling a primary amine to the  trisene core  and creating an intermediate the  monochlorotriazine intermediate  is then further substituted to a final  two-dimensional ligand  astria standardly creates an 8x8 array  library  of subtly different ligands this  building  of ligand libraries using a wide range  of different chemical compounds  allows for the creation of an extensive  library  incorporating a wide variety of  properties  which are costly stable  the libraries generated are pure plates  which are comprised of 64 discrete  packed  micro columns with top and bottom frits  and reservoirs above  these columns will drain freely under  gravity so  automated liquid handling systems are  not required  only a multi-channel pipette  these pureplate libraries allow us to  perform  the screening like using a standard  column  allowing for equilibration sample  application  wash steps elution and cip steps  the fractions of interest are collected  by drainage into deep well collection  plates  which can be analyzed using discrete  analytical tools  such as sds page or by a variety of  plate-based assays  to measure the amount of target protein  or impurities in each fraction  this allows for a variety of factors to  be identified  for the suitable ligand which binds to  the target  once the screening is complete the  fraction samples test  the fraction samples are tested using a  range of assays  the data can then be presented as a heat  map  such like here this heat map can be  built up  based on predetermined criteria such as  binding recovery purity ease evolution  or others from these criteria  the best adsorbents are selected a heap  map  can have a nominal scoring of one to  five  when the factors based on factors which  don't have a clear defined value  assigned  such as ease of evolution or there could  be  a percentage value out of a hundred  whereby the higher the value the better  in this case the poor performing ligands  are  colored in blue and the best performing  ligands are colored in red  from the screening we select one or more  absorbance  for further development or we decide  design a new library based on these  results and repeat the process  in this case as there were several  high-performing candidates  additional scrutiny was applied to the  results to identify the top candidates  in order to proceed to the absorbent  development  once the first phase is complete and one  or more usually up to six adsorbents  depending on what was identified during  ligand discovery  and these candidates have been  identified the next phase begins  absorbent development and process  development  the absorbent development can be  effectively divided into two parts  the chemistry development and the  downstream processing  the chemistry development focuses on  many aspects  including the optimization of the space  rom  the length and how the how hydrophobic  or how hydrophilic it needs to be  as mentioned earlier the ligand density  investing investigating a range of  ligand density absorbance  to explore selectivity and capacity  ligand attachment chemistry the base  matrix selection  i previously mentioned that we couple  the ligand to purebead  which is our near monodispersed  agarose-based matrix material  however other base matrices or even  other supports  can be investigated rational  optimization  of the ligand and absorbance synthesis  for improving cost effectiveness  and process robustness is also carried  out and  the list continues for the chemistry  development  the other part is the downstream  processing this focuses  on the development of a suitable wash  elution  regeneration and cip conditions as well  as testing the performance  of the absorbance under the standard  process conditions  the downstream processing will perform  all the verification work  in relation to the work which was  carried out throughout the chemistry  development  for the ligand and absorbent synthesis  the absorbent development uses scaled-up  synthesis  of the ligands which are packed into a  variety of larger columns  and are tested for performance there are  a key  there are a range of key factors at this  stage including as said  binding capacity selectivity elution  conditions  total protein binding impurity profiles  and recoveries  cip regimes are also investigated to  ensure that the adsorbent is stable  and reusable in this case  study sorry the um the example here  is after the optimization and the  development  on the on the right hand side is the sds  page of the ligand density optimization  we identified that a lower ligand  density was preferable  not only to improve the purity but also  to ensure good recovery  it's worth noting that although this  ligand showed  high selectivity for the target protein  certain charge groups present on the  ligand  did cause some minor non-target binding  which was accentuated  with the higher ligand densities  sometimes there is  a trade-off between purity and capacity  it's worth noting on the left where we  see the material  in the load and the final target in the  illusion  we were achieved 14 grams per liter  binding  with a 96 purity and a 98  recovery after the development and  optimization  once the absorbent has been fully  developed three  verification batches are produced  usually  at the one liter scale and are evaluated  for performance  and consistency within the limits of the  process  the process samples in the case in this  case study  were run on an sds page and compared  between the three batches demonstrating  high comparability  and illusion fractions with high purity  across the single step  once the adsorbent development is  complete the absorbent is ready for tech  transfer  into production at our manufacturing  facility on the island  during tech transfer all the documents  and reports required  are transferred to our manufacturing  site via our dedicated tt team  a single batch of adsorbent is produced  at the same  or larger scale than the verification  batches  the dt batch is fully evaluated and  assessed for comparability  to that of the verification batches for  the case study  an sds page of the tech transfer batch  was performed  and demonstrated comparability to the  verification batches  with a strong main target band visible  once the tech transfer batch has been  fully evaluated  manufacturer will commence creating at  least three  validation batches  during tech transfer and at the start of  manufacture  the equipment requirements and user  requirement specifications are evaluated  the incoming raw materials in process  assays  and final product assays for the product  are assessed for their suitability  and validated ligand and adsorbent  synthesis methods are validated  and all required standard operating  procedures and badge manufacturing  records  are established validation of the  adsorbent synthesis  is usually performed with the  manufacture of a minimum  of three batches usually at a larger  scale to that of the tt batch  demonstrating comparability and quality  the custom adsorbent is now a fully  validated and supported product  and can be supplied to the customer  however to fully support and enable the  customer  estrella also offers a column packing  service  if this is desired a packing development  program is initiated  to fully understand the packing  properties of the absorbent  and define all the parameters required  to produce  a uniform and robust packing process  this method follows the same route once  again  back through tech transfer and into  manufacture  along with our external testing  including transportation testing  we ensure that we can deliver a  plug-and-play play product  to the customer estrella also provide  smaller lab scale columns to perform  screening and process development  with their custom adsorbent if selected  or  one of a range of off-the-shelf products  for the case study discussed in this  presentation  we provided the customer with multiple  batches of pre-packed  evolved d seven and ten centimeter  diameter  gmp ready columns which are a perfect  fit for their requirements  we support our customers through each  stage from ligand discovery  to adsorbent and process development  once  a suitable candidate has been identified  and optimized  we undertake the tech transfer of a  robust process  for either adsorbent or column packing  services  we also provide a range of columns from  lab to process scale  through our technology people and our  processes  estrella has the capability to provide a  product or service which fits your needs  if anyone wants to find out more please  visit our website  at www.australiabuyseparations.com  thank you for your time and attention if  you have any questions regarding this  presentation  or any of our products or services  please please feel free to stop by our  booth or email sales at  australiahyphenbio.com


